starting the server and shaking hands: 1.7s, once

               median           range    tokens  tool calls
direct           6.0s        5.9-6.4s     1,654           2
over MCP         5.9s        5.3-6.6s     1,607           2

difference in median: -0.18s over 2 tool calls, so roughly -0.09s each
spread within a single version: 1.38s
-> the per-run difference is inside the noise

The token counts are the number that matters and they barely move: the model
is sent the same schemas and gets back the same results, because the adapter
hands the server's own descriptions straight to it. A protocol between your
agent and your tools is invisible to the thing you are paying by the token.

What you do pay is the handshake at the top of this output — a process start,
an index load and an initialize round trip — and you pay it per connection,
not per call. Open the session once and hold it, which is the only reason the
adapter in v0_11/mcp_client.py has a thread in it at all.

So the question is never overhead. It is whether you need the indirection:

  you do not    the tools live in your repository, ship with your code and
                are used by exactly one application
  you do        somebody else's application needs them, or somebody else's
                tools need you, and neither of you wants to be a library
